1
00:00:26,332 --> 00:00:33,655
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is a sutra in the Samyutta Nikaya called Malunkyaputra.

2
00:00:34,295 --> 00:00:37,876
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was an arahant at the end of his life, I think.

3
00:00:39,097 --> 00:00:44,359
[SPEAKER_00]: But in this sutra, he goes to the Buddha and asks him to teach, to get a teaching.

4
00:00:44,519 --> 00:00:46,539
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha asks him some questions.

5
00:00:48,623 --> 00:00:50,525
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, gives him instruction.

6
00:00:51,665 --> 00:01:01,433
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, Malankeya Putta responds with... with sort of a... in brief, to explain that he... how he understood what the Buddha... what it meant.

7
00:01:05,777 --> 00:01:09,580
[SPEAKER_00]: But I want to bring up this verse, which is the same in the Bahia Sutta.

8
00:01:11,461 --> 00:01:13,583
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha asks this monk,

9
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:15,541
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you think?

10
00:01:16,241 --> 00:01:31,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you have any desire, lust or affection for those forms cognizable by the eye, that are unseen by you, never seen before, that you don't see and that are not seen by you now?

11
00:01:33,896 --> 00:01:36,879
[SPEAKER_00]: So, do you have any desire or love for those?

12
00:01:36,979 --> 00:01:40,322
[SPEAKER_00]: The things that you haven't experienced with your senses, yeah.

13
00:01:40,362 --> 00:01:41,202
[SPEAKER_00]: And he replies, no.

14
00:01:44,396 --> 00:01:51,399
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's like the... what I said before, a number of times, it's like, which is more?

15
00:01:54,100 --> 00:02:00,202
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole point of that is, like, that reflection is to think, do you have lust for things?

16
00:02:00,402 --> 00:02:02,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have lust for things you haven't seen?

17
00:02:05,656 --> 00:02:06,536
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable.

18
00:02:07,017 --> 00:02:15,863
[SPEAKER_01]: So, having seen something is a prerequisite for lust, which means lust is not in seeing, not in the sight, not in the sight objects.

19
00:02:17,304 --> 00:02:20,667
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically you responsible for having lust towards that which is seen.

20
00:02:21,908 --> 00:02:27,972
[SPEAKER_01]: And one of the reasons why people would have lust towards that which is seen is because they take for granted that which is seen.

21
00:02:28,312 --> 00:02:30,074
[SPEAKER_01]: They take for granted their sense organs.

22
00:02:31,949 --> 00:02:36,271
[SPEAKER_01]: As their own, as owned, providers of pleasure and so on.

23
00:02:36,371 --> 00:02:39,233
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, whatever you see, it's the thing you see.

24
00:02:39,814 --> 00:02:41,355
[SPEAKER_01]: You're fully centered around that.

25
00:02:42,499 --> 00:02:56,208
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you actually stop and think about it, and doesn't matter what you see, what you have lost, the words, just kind of look at the context of that experience and see, well, okay, I've seen this, but which is more?

26
00:02:56,248 --> 00:02:59,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Things I haven't seen in my life or things I've seen?

27
00:02:59,790 --> 00:03:06,215
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize that things you've seen are always going to be inferior to everything else you haven't seen, everything else your eyes have not experienced.

28
00:03:08,091 --> 00:03:15,015
[SPEAKER_01]: They could be seen, but when they are seen, then they become that which is seen, which means there is always going to be more of that which is not seen.

29
00:03:16,056 --> 00:03:18,197
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you have lust for that which is not seen?

30
00:03:18,217 --> 00:03:19,198
[SPEAKER_01]: That's inconceivable.

31
00:03:19,698 --> 00:03:23,481
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the basis for non-lust is actually greater than the basis for lust.

32
00:03:25,782 --> 00:03:32,667
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, by thinking like this, you realize then that anything you have seen was purely circumstantial.

33
00:03:33,658 --> 00:03:35,459
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize, oh, I could have not seen it.

34
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:38,081
[SPEAKER_01]: It could have not been.

35
00:03:38,101 --> 00:03:39,621
[SPEAKER_01]: It was, but it could have not been.

36
00:03:39,661 --> 00:03:54,310
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anything else then, with that framework, with that established context, eh... anything else that you see now, you see against that kind of context of, oh, it could have not arisen, which means I didn't have said it's arising.

37
00:03:54,730 --> 00:04:02,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you're already much less centered around things you see, much less absorbed in them, which means much less, eh...

38
00:04:04,765 --> 00:04:09,728
[SPEAKER_01]: So, things you've seen are always secondary to things you haven't seen.

39
00:04:09,748 --> 00:04:13,690
[SPEAKER_01]: They're always circumstantial because you've seen them.

40
00:04:33,297 --> 00:04:35,478
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize your lust is always secondary.

41
00:04:36,398 --> 00:04:49,123
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to cultivate the foundation of just the recognition of everything that you haven't seen as a necessary basis for non-lust, it would be impossible for you to maintain the lust towards things you are seeing here and now.

42
00:04:51,084 --> 00:04:56,586
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're recognizing that non-desire, that non-lust in regard to

43
00:04:58,956 --> 00:05:12,530
[SPEAKER_01]: It all comes down to that practical, basically, undermining of that centeredness upon things you've seen, things you heard, things you taste, touched, and so on.

44
00:05:13,436 --> 00:05:21,098
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say centeredness, I mean that absorption in sense objects by default, like a default mode of being.

45
00:05:22,279 --> 00:05:35,783
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to basically... you want to sort of start eroding that gratuitous perception, and one of the ways is recognizing that anything you've seen, anything you will see, anything you're seeing now,

46
00:05:36,737 --> 00:05:46,680
[SPEAKER_01]: It's fundamentally circumstantial, because it's always against the backdrop of everything else not seen, everything else that you've never seen and hasn't been seen.

47
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:51,682
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that kind of puts the current lust towards things you see in a better perspective.

48
00:05:54,716 --> 00:06:02,180
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, it reminds me of the danger that I'm lusting after a sight or an experience.

49
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:04,661
[SPEAKER_00]: But this is a circumstantial thing.

50
00:06:04,721 --> 00:06:06,382
[SPEAKER_00]: And how much more experiences are there?

51
00:06:06,402 --> 00:06:06,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's the thing.

52
00:06:07,082 --> 00:06:08,523
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm lusting after...

53
00:06:08,643 --> 00:06:18,268
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm founding my entire being, my entire mental state, all the actions, choices that will then affect me in the future, upon something utterly circumstantial.

54
00:06:19,587 --> 00:06:42,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were to remember that what you are like currently, you're focused on the object of your sight, fully lustful, fully committed to get more of it, and then you develop context of its circumstantial nature against everything else that you haven't seen and you could have not seen, and you won't see, immediately there is already like a buffer zone between you and that gratuitous absorption with the sense objects.

55
00:06:46,249 --> 00:06:50,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, I've seen it and it's great, but did I have saying it's arising?

56
00:06:50,031 --> 00:06:51,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Did I have saying that seeing?

57
00:06:51,111 --> 00:06:52,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Did I have saying how that thing manifested?

58
00:06:52,732 --> 00:06:53,172
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean?

59
00:06:53,192 --> 00:06:53,372
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

60
00:06:53,872 --> 00:07:00,835
[SPEAKER_01]: You were just subjected to all of these things, yet you took them as if you were in control, you are the owner, you are the kind of... you hold the reins of it.

61
00:07:01,475 --> 00:07:02,215
[SPEAKER_01]: But, but you don't.

62
00:07:02,255 --> 00:07:08,938
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't hold the reins even in regard to your own senses, let alone that which comes through your senses to you, the sense objects.

63
00:07:09,659 --> 00:07:12,020
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, that there's so many more things to see.

64
00:07:13,120 --> 00:07:13,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

65
00:07:13,320 --> 00:07:14,501
[SPEAKER_00]: So many more things I could see.

66
00:07:14,761 --> 00:07:15,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's the thing.

67
00:07:15,642 --> 00:07:16,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Isn't that wonderful?

68
00:07:16,462 --> 00:07:21,204
[SPEAKER_01]: That, that thought would be on the level of you are still absorbed in the content of your seeing.

69
00:07:22,044 --> 00:07:27,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you actually develop the context of everything else that hasn't been seen is greater,

70
00:07:28,595 --> 00:07:33,476
[SPEAKER_01]: The thought of all of the other things I would see, wouldn't matter in its content, because you would see...

71
00:07:33,496 --> 00:07:38,657
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what I see in the future, it's always going to be secondary to the framework of everything else not seen.

72
00:07:39,818 --> 00:07:45,519
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you do lust for everything, the new things you could see, means you have lost the sight of the framework.

73
00:07:45,559 --> 00:07:47,339
[SPEAKER_01]: You have basically obscured the context.

74
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:51,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it doesn't matter what you see.

75
00:07:51,700 --> 00:07:55,041
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how many things you see, how many times you see it.

76
00:07:56,047 --> 00:07:58,409
[SPEAKER_01]: By being seen becomes second.

77
00:07:59,090 --> 00:08:00,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing it makes it second.

78
00:08:00,732 --> 00:08:04,555
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, oh, so there is no way for me to access it without seeing it.

79
00:08:05,516 --> 00:08:06,137
[SPEAKER_01]: With the lust.

80
00:08:07,258 --> 00:08:11,362
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I want to act out of lust, I have to make myself second to this.

81
00:08:12,042 --> 00:08:14,965
[SPEAKER_01]: By acting out of lust, you put the second as first.

82
00:08:15,466 --> 00:08:19,570
[SPEAKER_01]: You put the particular things that circumstantially have been seen,

83
00:08:20,472 --> 00:08:27,338
[SPEAKER_01]: You make that the center of your experience and your action and your choices, which means you then become dependent upon that which is already second.

84
00:08:28,059 --> 00:08:31,642
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the simile of the Buddha says chasing after sense objects.

85
00:08:32,022 --> 00:08:35,045
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like climbing the tree to eat the mangoes at the top.

86
00:08:35,685 --> 00:08:38,588
[SPEAKER_01]: And then somebody comes who can't climb the tree, but he can cut the tree.

87
00:08:39,108 --> 00:08:42,591
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when he starts cutting the tree, he's not cutting you.

88
00:08:43,071 --> 00:08:46,875
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not attacking you with the axe, but he's removing that upon which you stand.

89
00:08:47,772 --> 00:08:50,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Those sense-objects that you're currently committed to.

90
00:08:50,474 --> 00:08:53,636
[SPEAKER_01]: And unless you quickly withdraw yourself from those sense-objects, i.e.

91
00:08:53,676 --> 00:08:57,158
[SPEAKER_01]: quickly come down the tree, you will suffer death or deadly suffering.

92
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:04,642
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have lust, means you've already perverted the picture.

93
00:09:04,822 --> 00:09:07,644
[SPEAKER_01]: You've already put the second is first and first is second.

94
00:09:08,364 --> 00:09:10,165
[SPEAKER_01]: First is everything else non-seen.

95
00:09:10,285 --> 00:09:12,487
[SPEAKER_01]: First is the circumstantial nature of seen.

96
00:09:13,665 --> 00:09:22,904
[SPEAKER_01]: If you maintain that, doesn't matter what future seeing, what possibility of future seeing, it will all have to come second to the framework of everything else not seen.

97
00:09:24,087 --> 00:09:26,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly how you develop your mind above lust.

98
00:09:27,509 --> 00:09:31,891
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't develop your mind above lust by never perceiving anything beautiful in the world.

99
00:09:32,471 --> 00:09:37,113
[SPEAKER_01]: You develop your mind above lust by not being able to pervert that order ever again in the world.

100
00:09:37,253 --> 00:09:38,334
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what you see.

101
00:09:39,054 --> 00:09:43,336
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, what is... what is much more non-lust?

102
00:09:44,176 --> 00:09:48,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, from that point of view, you realize that basically the absence of lust is great.

103
00:09:48,898 --> 00:09:51,560
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it is possible to overcome centrality as...

104
00:09:53,122 --> 00:09:57,204
[SPEAKER_01]: As kind of hard as it is, it's still possible, because it's fundamentally second.

105
00:09:58,664 --> 00:10:05,347
[SPEAKER_01]: If sensuality were truly rooted in things you see, and the way you see them, it would be impossible, and it wouldn't be a problem, then.

106
00:10:05,607 --> 00:10:06,988
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't need to free yourself from it.

107
00:10:07,768 --> 00:10:11,789
[SPEAKER_01]: But the reason why sensuality is a problem, because it requires that perversion of that perception.

108
00:10:11,890 --> 00:10:13,050
[SPEAKER_01]: It requires distortion.

109
00:10:13,630 --> 00:10:15,171
[SPEAKER_01]: It requires the wrong view.

110
00:10:15,231 --> 00:10:20,393
[SPEAKER_01]: It requires implicit risk of committing to that which is circumstantial.

111
00:10:22,731 --> 00:10:27,512
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, like, imagine somebody offers you, you know, here's a car for you, drive it for free.

112
00:10:28,533 --> 00:10:30,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Brand new car, keys, never driven.

113
00:10:31,013 --> 00:10:33,014
[SPEAKER_01]: By the way, it might have a bomb dripped to it.

114
00:10:33,494 --> 00:10:34,714
[SPEAKER_01]: It might go off, it might not go off.

115
00:10:36,195 --> 00:10:36,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you risk it?

116
00:10:37,875 --> 00:10:41,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you risk it even touching the door handle?

117
00:10:42,656 --> 00:10:42,836
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

118
00:10:43,556 --> 00:10:44,437
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I'd rather walk.

119
00:10:44,797 --> 00:10:45,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

120
00:10:47,540 --> 00:10:49,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, here is a drink.

121
00:10:49,081 --> 00:10:50,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Enjoy.

122
00:10:50,481 --> 00:10:51,722
[SPEAKER_01]: It's beautiful in every regard.

123
00:10:52,742 --> 00:10:54,003
[SPEAKER_01]: It might be poisonous, by the way.

124
00:10:55,903 --> 00:10:56,904
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you wouldn't do it then.

125
00:10:56,944 --> 00:11:04,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, it doesn't matter the appeal of the sense object, how much you lasted for this type of car or for that type of drink.

126
00:11:05,310 --> 00:11:18,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, with the framework, with the context properly established, of, oh, this is basically establishing your own core upon something utterly circumstantial, that when it changes, it's not if, it's when it changes,

127
00:11:24,214 --> 00:11:31,818
[SPEAKER_01]: So, maintaining that context, doesn't matter what comes in your field of vision, you will not have lust towards it, because the peril will be apparent.

128
00:11:31,938 --> 00:11:34,520
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger becomes apparent, as we would say.

129
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:35,720
[SPEAKER_00]: You lose sight of that.

130
00:11:36,461 --> 00:11:37,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's mutually exclusive.

131
00:11:37,621 --> 00:11:46,406
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have the context of the danger, the risk, the circumstantial nature, and enjoy the object of that, which is circumstantial and risky.

132
00:11:47,487 --> 00:11:49,728
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way to enjoy it is to not see the risk.

133
00:11:51,564 --> 00:11:54,427
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter what I haven't seen, if I see it, it's nothing wrong.

134
00:11:54,447 --> 00:11:54,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

135
00:11:54,987 --> 00:11:56,708
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like I must never see it.

136
00:11:56,749 --> 00:12:02,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you will not seek things out, but if they do arise, you will not be overwhelmed or afraid of trying to avoid it either.

137
00:12:03,494 --> 00:12:06,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the scene, there will just be the scene.

138
00:12:06,817 --> 00:12:10,080
[SPEAKER_01]: There will be no avoiding it or trying to prolong nothing.

139
00:12:10,120 --> 00:12:15,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Just in the scene, there's just the scene, because in the scene, there is no more perversion of the context of the scene.

140
00:12:20,264 --> 00:12:21,045
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that verse.

141
00:12:22,145 --> 00:12:22,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

142
00:12:22,325 --> 00:12:27,950
[SPEAKER_00]: That the the Buddhist then gives, uh, Sri Lankan Buddha instructions how to train.

143
00:12:28,270 --> 00:12:29,811
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'll just read it out quickly.

144
00:12:29,831 --> 00:12:31,892
[SPEAKER_00]: There's obviously so many different translations.

145
00:12:31,932 --> 00:12:32,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

146
00:12:33,871 --> 00:12:41,056
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, when regarding things seen, heard, sensed and cognized by you, in the seen there will be merely the seen.

147
00:12:42,597 --> 00:12:44,338
[SPEAKER_00]: In the heard there will be merely the heard.

148
00:12:44,458 --> 00:12:46,459
[SPEAKER_00]: In the sensed there will be merely the sensed.

149
00:12:46,519 --> 00:12:48,661
[SPEAKER_00]: In the cognized there will be merely the cognized.

150
00:12:49,381 --> 00:12:52,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you will not be by that.

151
00:12:53,064 --> 00:12:54,525
[SPEAKER_00]: That by which you're defined.

152
00:12:55,385 --> 00:12:58,948
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be... basically, you won't... that's what I mean, like, when you establish your being,

153
00:13:00,245 --> 00:13:03,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Upon that lust towards the seen, it defines you.

154
00:13:04,208 --> 00:13:08,612
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, your ownership, things you have committed to emotionally, things you...

155
00:13:08,997 --> 00:13:13,500
[SPEAKER_01]: You crave for things you want to have, things you want to protect having, and so on.

156
00:13:13,881 --> 00:13:14,942
[SPEAKER_01]: That is what defines you.

157
00:13:15,182 --> 00:13:17,283
[SPEAKER_01]: That is what your sense of self is.

158
00:13:17,303 --> 00:13:18,784
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's that sutta where the Buddha says that.

159
00:13:19,145 --> 00:13:25,289
[SPEAKER_01]: That King Mahāsābhū Muṣayyānā... Basically, he said he was defined by his possession.

160
00:13:25,309 --> 00:13:31,014
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, if that king wouldn't have the king's horses, king's wives, king's palaces, king's army, would he be the king?

161
00:13:31,994 --> 00:13:33,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Could he act as a king?

162
00:13:33,255 --> 00:13:33,416
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

163
00:13:35,177 --> 00:13:37,619
[SPEAKER_01]: But because he has these things, that's why he is the king.

164
00:13:38,762 --> 00:13:43,783
[SPEAKER_01]: But, in the mind that is already perverted with this lust, you think you are the king first.

165
00:13:45,224 --> 00:13:48,965
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the most fundamental distortion that Puthujjana does.

166
00:13:49,385 --> 00:13:51,985
[SPEAKER_01]: You think things are yours, because you are.

167
00:13:52,766 --> 00:13:53,806
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually the other way around.

168
00:13:54,486 --> 00:13:54,946
[SPEAKER_01]: You are.

169
00:13:55,126 --> 00:14:00,208
[SPEAKER_01]: You have a sense of self, for whatever it is, because you keep owning things gratuitously.

170
00:14:00,308 --> 00:14:03,208
[SPEAKER_01]: And you keep owning them, because you don't see them as circumstantial.

171
00:14:03,828 --> 00:14:05,489
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see them as subject to change.

172
00:14:06,585 --> 00:14:16,950
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even when the change happens, blatantly in your face, you don't see that as a relevant symptom, or that undermines your ownership, because you assumed yourself to come first.

173
00:14:17,511 --> 00:14:29,157
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you then just isolate these examples when change goes against you, and undermines your ownership, and undermines your sense of self, and then just try to cover it up with other things, getting the new property, and new ownership, and new possessions.

174
00:14:29,839 --> 00:14:38,604
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where sensuality, basically a form of owning and consumption, is the only way that Kutuja knows how to deal with any discomfort emotionally that he experiences.

175
00:14:38,704 --> 00:14:46,930
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't know how to escape the painful feeling in any other way except turning towards sensuality and trying to basically cover it up with new stuff.

176
00:14:48,290 --> 00:14:50,992
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact is that things could change against your will.

177
00:14:51,532 --> 00:14:53,434
[SPEAKER_01]: It means that fundamentally they cannot be owned.

178
00:14:53,854 --> 00:14:58,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you are owning them now, it's not the ownership that you assume you have.

179
00:14:59,508 --> 00:15:02,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the ownership of control and mastery over these things.

180
00:15:02,249 --> 00:15:10,211
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you see the circumstantial nature of these... By dwelling, basically, in that circumstantial context, you will be unable to not include yourself in it as well.

181
00:15:10,791 --> 00:15:12,431
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to start feeling it, in other words.

182
00:15:12,991 --> 00:15:26,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start contemplating the circumstantial nature of your possessions, and sooner or later, you're going to feel a pang of... of... of dukkha, basically, when you touch upon something that's dear to you, and now you start contemplating it as perishable.

183
00:15:27,475 --> 00:15:28,395
[SPEAKER_01]: And then it's just, oh,

184
00:15:29,215 --> 00:15:32,357
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you yourself are equally subject to these things.

185
00:15:32,637 --> 00:15:34,779
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the body is the closest, isn't it?

186
00:15:34,799 --> 00:15:43,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the body is the closest, yeah, but it's also the hardest one to see, because through the body you get the pleasure upon which you develop yourself as dependent upon the sense objects.

187
00:15:43,764 --> 00:15:46,286
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't even see the body correctly, in other words.

188
00:15:46,826 --> 00:15:48,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Human people say, yes, the body is anicca.

189
00:15:49,068 --> 00:15:52,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you feel anxiety and fear on account of it, you're not doing it rightly.

190
00:15:53,230 --> 00:15:54,571
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not seeing the anicca of the body.

191
00:15:54,711 --> 00:15:55,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you might be an arahant.

192
00:15:56,664 --> 00:16:00,125
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, you wouldn't be listening to these instructions if you were.

193
00:16:00,426 --> 00:16:01,666
[SPEAKER_01]: So, more likely, you're not.

194
00:16:02,466 --> 00:16:03,167
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the point.

195
00:16:04,007 --> 00:16:12,851
[SPEAKER_01]: You, your sense of self, your sense of everything, basically, ownerships and views and everything, it's because you possess things first.

196
00:16:13,913 --> 00:16:20,499
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because, you know, every single individual time you engage with the objects of your senses, you do so ignorantly.

197
00:16:20,619 --> 00:16:22,281
[SPEAKER_01]: You do so without the right context.

198
00:16:22,741 --> 00:16:27,666
[SPEAKER_01]: You do so without maintaining the recognition that they're circumstantial throughout.

199
00:16:27,786 --> 00:16:29,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in the beginning, but now it's final.

200
00:16:29,888 --> 00:16:33,732
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning, in the middle, in the end, doesn't matter what point you engage with things,

201
00:16:37,615 --> 00:16:46,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, you give possessions by correcting the distortion that these possessions have... that the lust towards the possession have basically maintained.

202
00:16:50,686 --> 00:16:52,147
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the scene, there's just the scene.

203
00:16:52,387 --> 00:16:53,248
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no distortion.

204
00:16:53,769 --> 00:16:54,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Clear.

205
00:16:57,412 --> 00:17:02,516
[SPEAKER_01]: But for somebody without the right context, in the scene, well...

206
00:17:03,121 --> 00:17:08,944
[SPEAKER_01]: There is lust, there is sense of self, there is ownership, there is basically perception of permanence, there's everything.

207
00:17:09,264 --> 00:17:09,884
[SPEAKER_01]: Just in the scene.

208
00:17:11,545 --> 00:17:13,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Towards, or... Yeah, exactly.

209
00:17:13,666 --> 00:17:15,067
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they're defined by the scene.

210
00:17:16,768 --> 00:17:17,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Or they're in the scene.

211
00:17:18,309 --> 00:17:19,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Or they're different from the scene.

212
00:17:19,569 --> 00:17:23,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, there's all this mess caused by lust towards the scene.

213
00:17:24,332 --> 00:17:26,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by the scene, by the way.

214
00:17:26,553 --> 00:17:31,616
[SPEAKER_00]: When you are not by that, then you will not be there, therein.

215
00:17:32,801 --> 00:17:40,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's basically depend... living... pretty much seeing yourself as... in the same world of all these objects that you own.

216
00:17:40,963 --> 00:17:42,783
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just proliferation of that self-view.

217
00:17:44,624 --> 00:17:47,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's... it can kind of grow infinitely.

218
00:17:47,484 --> 00:17:48,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

219
00:17:49,345 --> 00:18:02,208
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through owning, through being defined by... pretty much objects of your senses, by things you see, smell, taste, touch, by chasing the pleasure of these things, you become a being that's in the world of these sense objects.

220
00:18:03,078 --> 00:18:16,005
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why a thought of sense restraint, a thought of not chasing the sensual pleasure, first, and if, like, in the beginning, if the mind is not trained, will fill you with anxiety and terror.

221
00:18:16,025 --> 00:18:16,966
[SPEAKER_01]: This will kill me.

222
00:18:17,226 --> 00:18:18,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

223
00:18:18,267 --> 00:18:21,528
[SPEAKER_01]: This will kill the level of me that I proliferated to.

224
00:18:22,169 --> 00:18:22,869
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I'm saying.

225
00:18:22,889 --> 00:18:27,452
[SPEAKER_01]: People, like, even people who are not into practice, when they get, like, you know, you chase sensuality,

226
00:18:28,269 --> 00:18:38,613
[SPEAKER_01]: To the point of, basically, if you don't meet the required threshold of engagement with your sense objects, you start experiencing, basically, well, very painful psychological states.

227
00:18:40,674 --> 00:18:41,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Loss of existence.

228
00:18:41,434 --> 00:18:42,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, exactly.

229
00:18:42,054 --> 00:18:44,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Like vacuum, and dread, and fear, and all this.

230
00:18:45,335 --> 00:18:49,857
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you quickly need to find activities, and things to do, and things to keep yourself occupied.

231
00:18:50,929 --> 00:19:06,020
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, equally, if you actually are able to not, kind of, give in to that anxiety and be overwhelmed by it, and endure it, and refuse to engage with the sense object to the same extent, you are diminishing that gratuitously proliferated being in that world of sense objects.

232
00:19:07,320 --> 00:19:12,264
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can do so by maintaining the context of the circumstantial nature towards things you want to engage with.

233
00:19:13,465 --> 00:19:14,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's another thing.

234
00:19:14,846 --> 00:19:17,628
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were, oh, I need to do this, I need to do this,

235
00:19:18,529 --> 00:19:25,814
[SPEAKER_01]: If you re-establish the context of circumstantial nature, even of the possibilities of things you could do, you already lost the lust of doing them.

236
00:19:26,454 --> 00:19:31,198
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you'll end up just doing things you had to do, things that are practical in nature, that are required, whatever.

237
00:19:32,178 --> 00:19:34,180
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be doing them out of lust, in other words.

238
00:19:36,324 --> 00:19:38,848
[SPEAKER_00]: That verse continues.

239
00:19:38,948 --> 00:19:44,837
[SPEAKER_00]: When you are not therein, then you will be neither here nor beyond nor in between the two.

240
00:19:46,921 --> 00:19:49,224
[SPEAKER_00]: This itself is the end of suffering.

241
00:19:51,418 --> 00:19:53,782
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the instruction to Malankyaputta.

242
00:19:53,962 --> 00:20:02,674
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, Malankyaputta says to the Buddha, I understand in detail, Venerable Sir, the meaning of what was stated by the Blessed One in brief.

243
00:20:03,055 --> 00:20:06,320
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is his verse, which is also found in the Theragata.

244
00:20:07,001 --> 00:20:07,502
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's see.

245
00:20:10,521 --> 00:20:25,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Having seen a form with mindfulness confused, attending to the pleasing sign... Having seen the form with context confused... With recollectedness lost...

246
00:20:25,267 --> 00:20:32,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Without the right context, without the right recollectedness, having seen the form without a context, you will be confused with it.

247
00:20:32,170 --> 00:20:34,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're already putting it first.

248
00:20:37,800 --> 00:20:38,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

249
00:20:38,200 --> 00:20:42,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you're already pulled by pleasure.

250
00:20:44,085 --> 00:20:51,130
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only, as I said in the beginning of this talk, the way to not be pulled by pleasure is not to never see things, but to never lose the context.

251
00:20:52,251 --> 00:20:54,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Because context means recognition of the right order.

252
00:20:55,675 --> 00:20:58,436
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is already lust, it means there is absence of context.

253
00:20:59,036 --> 00:21:00,157
[SPEAKER_01]: You always start with lust.

254
00:21:00,437 --> 00:21:01,717
[SPEAKER_01]: You always start with perversion.

255
00:21:02,138 --> 00:21:02,918
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a mode of being.

256
00:21:03,558 --> 00:21:07,280
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it takes effort of going against that grain to develop the right context.

257
00:21:07,340 --> 00:21:15,163
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, sure, the point will come where it will be a matter of choice of a clearer kind, where you can clearly see mindfulness of lust or lust.

258
00:21:15,623 --> 00:21:18,524
[SPEAKER_00]: And one experiences it with an infatuated mind.

259
00:21:18,784 --> 00:21:19,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

260
00:21:19,405 --> 00:21:21,165
[SPEAKER_00]: And remains holding to it.

261
00:21:21,326 --> 00:21:22,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, assuming it.

262
00:21:23,066 --> 00:21:24,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Assuming it in that wrong order.

263
00:21:27,513 --> 00:21:34,155
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

264
00:21:34,175 --> 00:21:39,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, your mode of being becomes dependent upon the sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

265
00:21:39,617 --> 00:21:46,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means now, everything you start feeling, experiencing feelings on account of it, is now on top of that.

266
00:21:46,539 --> 00:21:48,320
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're just proliferating more and more.

267
00:21:50,197 --> 00:22:00,501
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, every... you know, any sensual... basically, if there is no... if sensuality is not kept in check, for most people, by circumstances or by limited access to it, everybody would be an addict.

268
00:22:03,922 --> 00:22:05,582
[SPEAKER_01]: And most people are, one way or the other.

269
00:22:06,823 --> 00:22:07,343
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

270
00:22:07,363 --> 00:22:08,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's not a static thing.

271
00:22:08,643 --> 00:22:12,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I'll just enjoy sensuality and then I'll restrain myself.

272
00:22:12,405 --> 00:22:12,585
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

273
00:22:12,765 --> 00:22:16,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Each time you enjoy sensuality, means next time you'll need to enjoy more of it.

274
00:22:17,226 --> 00:22:18,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Your being grows towards it.

275
00:22:20,938 --> 00:22:22,780
[SPEAKER_01]: You're becoming more dependent upon it.

276
00:22:23,701 --> 00:22:28,006
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not a static thing.

277
00:22:29,747 --> 00:22:31,690
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the danger you have to recognize as well.

278
00:22:32,633 --> 00:22:35,975
[SPEAKER_01]: When people think, okay, no, I understand this, I'll restrain later.

279
00:22:36,896 --> 00:22:39,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Later might be too late, as it might be too much.

280
00:22:40,218 --> 00:22:45,441
[SPEAKER_01]: You only have maybe a certain limited of capacity to withdraw yourself from it.

281
00:22:46,462 --> 00:22:51,905
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you overflow that being of sensuality, you will not have means of basically digging yourself out of it anymore.

282
00:22:56,625 --> 00:23:06,875
[SPEAKER_00]: So, many feelings grow, originating from that sight, covetousness and annoyance as well, by which one's mind becomes disturbed.

283
00:23:07,056 --> 00:23:07,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

284
00:23:07,796 --> 00:23:14,002
[SPEAKER_00]: For one who accumulates suffering like this, nibbāna is said to be far away.

285
00:23:14,043 --> 00:23:14,723
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

286
00:23:15,424 --> 00:23:15,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

287
00:23:15,784 --> 00:23:19,328
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just more and more and more just piling things up.

288
00:23:22,407 --> 00:23:30,672
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, even with people who... a person, with all that piled up on him, thinks about nibbāna, he will think it in terms of that pile, basically.

289
00:23:31,232 --> 00:23:41,559
[SPEAKER_01]: For him, nibbāna will be another central thing, the greatest fulfillment of desire, the greatest bliss, the greatest pleasure, which is just the extension of everything else he's been chasing so far.

290
00:23:42,099 --> 00:23:49,263
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean, like when I said before, nibbāna is closer to death for a kutujana than basically that which he thinks nibbāna is.

291
00:23:50,324 --> 00:23:51,505
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, again,

292
00:23:52,548 --> 00:24:04,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Many, numerous times in the suttas, when the Buddha would talk to lay people, some of them would ask, and he would give them the description of what nibbāna is, and they would go completely distraught.

293
00:24:05,102 --> 00:24:07,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolute breakdown, mental breakdown, fall on the ground.

294
00:24:08,766 --> 00:24:09,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Agony pulling me.

295
00:24:09,507 --> 00:24:11,308
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, oh, that will kill me, that will destroy me.

296
00:24:11,668 --> 00:24:13,989
[SPEAKER_01]: That liberation is the death of me.

297
00:24:14,089 --> 00:24:14,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is.

298
00:24:15,330 --> 00:24:17,911
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the death of your sense of self, i.e.

299
00:24:17,951 --> 00:24:22,554
[SPEAKER_01]: proliferated being upon sense objects, feelings on account of it, cravings and so on.

300
00:24:22,754 --> 00:24:23,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumptions, of course.

301
00:24:26,116 --> 00:24:30,018
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, that's why the true practice is more...

302
00:24:31,119 --> 00:24:35,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically speaking, more often than not, it would be unpleasant for people, certainly in the beginning.

303
00:24:36,093 --> 00:24:38,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's going to be about undoing your sense of self.

304
00:24:39,075 --> 00:24:46,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Not finding that last missing bit that will make you full and completed into this ultimate joy of nibbāna.

305
00:24:47,059 --> 00:24:47,319
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

306
00:24:47,479 --> 00:24:51,461
[SPEAKER_01]: It's undoing that whole stinky pile of sensual rubbish.

307
00:24:54,503 --> 00:24:58,585
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more you accumulate, the more work you have to basically do to clean it up.

308
00:25:02,213 --> 00:25:05,075
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the carelessness, you know, it goes by carelessness.

309
00:25:05,115 --> 00:25:07,558
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically, fundamentally, procrastination.

310
00:25:09,319 --> 00:25:22,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Like people who read the suttas, recognize what needs to be done, but then keep, sort of, absolving themselves from the sense of duty to do it, or kind of postponing it, or later, wait for the right condition, wait for this, wait for this.

311
00:25:22,530 --> 00:25:25,192
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just procrastinating, which means it's not a static thing.

312
00:25:25,593 --> 00:25:28,115
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your procrastination, for as long as it lasts,

313
00:25:28,575 --> 00:25:32,857
[SPEAKER_01]: Results in accumulation of more things for that period of time that you then have to clean.

314
00:25:33,437 --> 00:25:38,759
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you couldn't make yourself clean your room when it was barely cluttered, because you were procrastinating.

315
00:25:39,319 --> 00:25:44,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, because of your procrastination, you have hundred times more things to clean.

316
00:25:44,942 --> 00:25:45,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Good luck starting now.

317
00:25:48,883 --> 00:25:50,124
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the burden accumulates.

318
00:25:50,404 --> 00:25:51,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's been your practice.

319
00:25:52,424 --> 00:25:52,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

320
00:25:53,045 --> 00:25:54,105
[SPEAKER_01]: That's been your moral being.

321
00:25:55,626 --> 00:25:56,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Postponing it.

322
00:26:03,104 --> 00:26:04,605
[SPEAKER_00]: And it continues with, eh...

323
00:26:06,747 --> 00:26:17,657
[SPEAKER_00]: When recollected, when you have that context, mindfulness, one sees a form, one is not inflamed by lust for that sight.

324
00:26:18,778 --> 00:26:23,001
[SPEAKER_00]: One experiences it with a dispassionate mind and does not remain holding.

325
00:26:26,191 --> 00:26:37,719
[SPEAKER_00]: One lives recollected, mindful, in such a way, that even as one sees the form, and while one undergoes a feeling, suffering is diminished, not built up.

326
00:26:38,280 --> 00:26:43,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's no craving that's being basically cultivated towards it.

327
00:26:43,183 --> 00:26:48,167
[SPEAKER_01]: You prioritize the context, not the content of your experience.

328
00:26:49,778 --> 00:26:50,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, that's what it is.

329
00:26:51,239 --> 00:26:56,501
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and for one dismantling suffering like that, nibbāna is said to be close by.

330
00:26:56,621 --> 00:26:57,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

331
00:26:57,121 --> 00:26:59,722
[SPEAKER_01]: He's already peeling off all these layers.

332
00:27:02,323 --> 00:27:06,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a skilled butcher cutting up a cow, as the Buddha would describe.

333
00:27:07,505 --> 00:27:08,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Cleaning and cleaning.

334
00:27:08,246 --> 00:27:08,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

335
00:27:08,386 --> 00:27:09,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Cleaning and cleaning and cleaning.

336
00:27:09,686 --> 00:27:10,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Polishing.

337
00:27:10,246 --> 00:27:10,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Polishing.

338
00:27:12,407 --> 00:27:13,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the thing.

339
00:27:13,328 --> 00:27:16,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Even, like, even almost like a mundane level of practice,

340
00:27:18,522 --> 00:27:19,903
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

341
00:27:20,223 --> 00:27:22,064
[SPEAKER_01]: You see somebody and it's upsetting to you.

342
00:27:22,924 --> 00:27:25,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you see somebody and you're infected with lust.

343
00:27:25,305 --> 00:27:25,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

344
00:27:27,006 --> 00:27:30,047
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, the context is, there is a lustful mind.

345
00:27:30,948 --> 00:27:32,929
[SPEAKER_01]: The context is, there is an angry mind.

346
00:27:33,069 --> 00:27:34,329
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter who it is.

347
00:27:35,390 --> 00:27:40,652
[SPEAKER_01]: The content of that experience is the person you're angry with, or the person you're lustful towards.

348
00:27:41,032 --> 00:27:42,113
[SPEAKER_01]: So you always have a choice.

349
00:27:43,085 --> 00:27:43,525
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

350
00:27:43,565 --> 00:27:52,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensory strength, fundamentally, is done blindly, but it actually... the reason why it's necessary and why it's wholesome, because it emulates the nibbāna.

351
00:27:55,148 --> 00:27:57,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of sensuality is what nibbāna is.

352
00:27:57,709 --> 00:27:59,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of possibility of lust is what nibbāna is.

353
00:28:00,510 --> 00:28:03,511
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is basically outlined through sensory strength.

354
00:28:04,291 --> 00:28:11,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, of course, you need to develop understanding through that sensory strength, but without sensory strength, there is no place for understanding to occur.

355
00:28:13,629 --> 00:28:37,899
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even on that mundane level, you have a choice whether to, like, leap into the content of your experience, and he is the reason of my anger, or think about him, because you are angry, or abstain from giving in to that pull, to that burden that's kind of weighing on you to go that way, and stay on the level of the context, which is angry mind endures.

356
00:28:40,476 --> 00:28:44,547
[SPEAKER_01]: If you lapse from the context, you find yourself thinking about the person you're angry with.

357
00:28:44,567 --> 00:28:45,831
[SPEAKER_01]: You re-establish the context.

358
00:28:46,172 --> 00:28:46,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger is there.

359
00:28:47,034 --> 00:28:48,518
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't care on account of what.

360
00:28:49,488 --> 00:28:54,471
[SPEAKER_01]: You're already not particularly acting towards craving and feeling and aversion towards person you hate.

361
